{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"control-flow","title":"Control Flow","group":"Language Basics","order":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>if</code>, <code>switch</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>do-while</code> — plus the modern additions: range-based <code>for</code>, <code>if</code>/<code>switch</code> with an initializer, and <code>[[fallthrough]]</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-branch\"></i><code>if</code> / <code>else</code> and the ternary</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;map&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int score = 72;\n    if (score &gt;= 90)      std::cout &lt;&lt; \"A\\n\";\n    else if (score &gt;= 70) std::cout &lt;&lt; \"B\\n\";\n    else                  std::cout &lt;&lt; \"C\\n\";\n\n    std::string parity = (score % 2 == 0) ? \"even\" : \"odd\";\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; parity &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::map&lt;std::string, int&gt; stock{{\"apple\", 3}};\n    if (auto it = stock.find(\"apple\"); it != stock.end())   // C++17: init; condition\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"apples: \" &lt;&lt; it-&gt;second &lt;&lt; '\\n';      // `it` is scoped to the if/else\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>B\neven\napples: 3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-switch\"></i><code>switch</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nvoid describe(char op) {\n    switch (op) {\n        case '+':\n        case '-':\n            std::cout &lt;&lt; op &lt;&lt; \" is additive\\n\";\n            break;\n        case '*':\n            std::cout &lt;&lt; \"* is multiplicative\\n\";\n            [[fallthrough]];              // intentional: silences the warning\n        case '/':\n            std::cout &lt;&lt; \"  (binary operator)\\n\";\n            break;\n        default:\n            std::cout &lt;&lt; op &lt;&lt; \" unknown\\n\";\n    }\n}\nint main() { describe('-'); describe('*'); describe('?'); }</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>- is additive\n* is multiplicative\n  (binary operator)\n? unknown</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>switch</code> works only on integral and enum types (not <code>std::string</code>), and a missing <code>break</code> falls through into the next case. You can't declare an initialised variable across case labels without braces: <code>case 1: { int x = 5; … } break;</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>Loops</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{3, 1, 4, 1, 5};\n    for (std::size_t i = 0; i &lt; v.size(); ++i) std::cout &lt;&lt; v[i];   // classic index loop\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    for (int x : v) std::cout &lt;&lt; x * 2 &lt;&lt; ' ';                       // range-based for\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    for (int&amp; x : v) x += 10;                                        // reference: modify in place\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; v.front() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    int n = 1;\n    while (n &lt; 100) n *= 3;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    int tries = 0;\n    do { ++tries; } while (tries &lt; 0);   // body runs at least once\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; tries &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>31415\n6 2 8 2 10\n13\n243\n1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-skip-forward\"></i><code>break</code>, <code>continue</code> and nested loops</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 10; ++i) {\n        if (i % 2 == 0) continue;   // skip evens\n        if (i &gt; 7) break;           // stop the loop\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    bool found = false;              // leaving nested loops: use a flag (or a function + return)\n    for (int r = 0; r &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; !found; ++r)\n        for (int c = 0; c &lt; 3; ++c)\n            if (r * c == 2) { std::cout &lt;&lt; r &lt;&lt; ',' &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; '\\n'; found = true; break; }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 3 5 7\n1,2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Loop</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>for (init; cond; step)</code></td><td>You need the index or a custom step</td></tr><tr><td><code>for (auto&amp; x : range)</code></td><td>Visit every element — the default choice</td></tr><tr><td><code>while (cond)</code></td><td>Number of iterations isn't known up front</td></tr><tr><td><code>do { } while (cond);</code></td><td>Body must run at least once (e.g. menu loops)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How does the range-based for loop work under the hood?</summary><div><p><code>for (auto x : r) body</code> is rewritten to roughly <code>auto&amp;&amp; __r = r; for (auto it = begin(__r), e = end(__r); it != e; ++it) { auto x = *it; body }</code>. So it works for any type with <code>begin()</code>/<code>end()</code> — arrays, all STL containers, <code>std::string</code>, ranges and your own classes.</p></div></details></section>"}